{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792251480,"nonce":"AzfxdVtIjmmsTlvLPGvrW3i1B60=","title":"176 integration-test binaries each link the whole stack, bloating target/","message":"Every file under a crate's `tests/` is its own binary, so a test run links the crate's dependency stack once per file: 61 in blinc_cn_dsl (with the zyntax compiler and cranelift), 39 in blinc_cn, 32 in blinc_dsl_core, 31 in blinc_layout, and a few elsewhere. With incremental already off and the dev profile already lean (line tables for workspace crates, no dependency debuginfo), these links and the stale copies left by differing feature sets are what grow target/.\n\nDone: blinc_cn_dsl, blinc_dsl_core, blinc_gpu and blinc_text are one `integration` binary each (`autotests = false`, tests/main.rs declaring every file as a module): 102 binaries become 4. Their nextest results are unchanged (the DSL crates keep the same 28 known failures), and gpu and text also pass under threaded `cargo test`.\n\nNot done: blinc_layout, blinc_cn and blinc_router. Merged, they pass under nextest (one process per test) but fail under threaded `cargo test`: tests from different files share process-wide state (the reactive graph, the pending-update queues, pointer state) that each file only guards within itself. CI's main job uses nextest; the Android workflow's \"Run tests\" step reruns the host suite with plain `cargo test --workspace`, the one threaded consumer. Merging these needs either that step moved to nextest, or a lock shared across the merged binary for tests that touch global state.\n\nAlso: the local pre-commit gate ran its threaded step without `--all-features` while nextest used it, building a second copy of the affected crates; both now use it.","files":null}]}